From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] SystemUI: a notification is swiped away by the finger

Patch 0055 put the question on the device. With `log.tag.swipe DEBUG` set and
a clearable notification, one swipe of 514 px to the right answered it:

    mTranslation= 312.6834  mAlpha= 0.0  velocity= 0.0
    minDismissVel= 937.5  isDismissGesture= false  gestureTowardsMenu= false
    gestureFastEnough= false  gestureFarEnough= false

Both halves of `isDismissGesture()` read values that this panel, in these
measurements, did not produce.

  * **Distance.** `swipedFarEnough()` compares the **view's** translation with
    `SWIPED_FAR_ENOUGH_SIZE_FRACTION * getSize()`, 0.6 * 1072 = 643 px here.
    The view had reached 312.7 px of a 514 px swipe, and the missing 201 px
    are not frames that were too slow to draw: when dragging begins,
    `onInterceptTouchEvent` moves `mInitialTouchPos` to **where the finger is
    at that moment** and the drag measures from there. On a phone that costs
    the touch slop, 8 dp. Here a move is processed about every 190 ms while
    the shade is being dragged (`dumpsys input`: `age=189.3ms, wait=187.7ms`),
    so the first one that gets through is already far along, and everything
    before it is thrown away.
  * **Speed.** `swipedFastEnough()` reads `VelocityTracker`, whose strategy
    keeps only samples from the last 100 ms. On this screen the reader drags,
    waits to see what happened, and then lifts; by the lift there is nothing
    in the window, so the velocity is 0.0 against a 937.5 px/s threshold.

Across what 0055 tried -- 150, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 900 and 1200 ms over
850 to 1040 px, both directions -- no swipe ever dismissed one. The row
follows the finger, falls behind, and snaps back. 「全部清除」 still works,
which is why the removal path itself was never in doubt.

Three things change, and they are one decision: **the gesture is the finger's,
from where it landed, and it takes effect when the finger lifts.**

1.  `mGestureInitialTouchPos` and `mGestureInitialTranslation` are set at the
    touch down and moved by nothing else until the next one, and
    `mGestureTranslation` is recomputed from them on every event, so the travel
    before the first processed move is kept. Both predicates,
    the translation handed to `handleUpEvent`, and the direction `dismissChild`
    throws the row in now read them -- that last one because the view is no
    longer moved, so its own translation is 0 and a slow swipe to the left,
    the ordinary speed here, would have thrown the row off to the right.
    `mTouchAboveFalsingThreshold` is updated in the same place, so a gesture
    whose moves were starved is no longer treated as a false touch.

2.  The row is no longer moved under the finger and its swipe progress is no
    longer updated on every move, so the shade paints nothing of the gesture
    until the lift (the menu row still gets each move, and decides at the lift). This is rule 6 of `docs/design/eink-ui.md` -- 「大区域的手势在手指抬
    起时一步生效」 -- the rule Launcher3 0006 already applies to opening and
    closing the app list. Following a finger at 134 ms a frame only produces a
    string of half-drawn pictures, and the per-move `invalidateGlobalRegion()`
    up the whole view hierarchy is work this shade cannot afford.
    `mCanCurrViewBeDimissed` existed only to feed that call, and goes with it.

3.  `SWIPED_FAR_ENOUGH_SIZE_FRACTION` becomes 0.4. AOSP can ask for 0.6 of the
    width because a short fast fling dismisses as well; with that path not
    reached in any of the measurements above, the distance has to carry the
    whole gesture, and 643 px is 54 mm of a 90 mm
    screen. 0.4 is not a new number: `NotificationMenuRow.CheckForDrag` already
    stops offering the menu at `mParent.getWidth() * 0.4f`, because past that
    the gesture is a dismissal and not a reveal. Holding the two at 0.6 and 0.4
    leaves a band -- 429 to 643 px -- where neither happens, which on a phone
    the fling covers and here is dead travel. At 0.4 the bands meet: a short
    swipe reveals the menu, a long one removes the notification.

Because the row no longer moves under the finger, `NotificationMenuRow`'s copy
of the translation -- fed from `ExpandableNotificationRow.setTranslation` --
would stay at 0 for the whole gesture, and its menu could never be revealed
again. It is given the gesture's translation instead, at the touch down, on
each move and at the lift, so its own decision still works and its result,
like the dismissal, lands in one step when the finger comes up.

This also applies to Recents, which uses the same helper: its cards stop
following the finger and are dismissed at 0.4 of a card instead of 0.6.

